# Spec - tb-convergence-sweep-records

**Feature:** `tb-convergence-sweep-records`
**Created:** 2026-11-26
**Issue:** #144 (tracking #150); folds in #134
**Design:** [`design.md`](design.md), [`interview.md`](interview.md) D1-D8

## Summary

An implementer's fix claim carries a sweep record: the defect class, every
site with the search that found them, the fix, the class-level test and the
dependents. GateReeve enforces the record's presence and shape, gives the
open review scope's records to every later boundary reviewer, and treats a
later finding of a swept class as a regression of the previous fix. A peer's
re-reported regression keeps its origin, and individual reviews of one round
are judged together (#134).

## Definition of Done

The global Definition of Done in the software development workflow applies.
In addition: the CLI test suite passes, and the desktop protocol mirror
(`apps/desktop/resources/protocol`) is byte-identical to
`plugin-src/shared/resources/protocol`.

## Acceptance Criteria

- **AC1. Claim disposition.** Every entry of `claims` (in `gate.execute` and
  `review.execute`) has `findingId`, `claim` and `disposition`, which is
  `fixed` or `disputed`, and may have `sweep`; no other key is accepted. An
  entry without `disposition`, or with another value, is refused naming
  `claims[i].disposition` and the allowed values. A `fixed` entry without
  `sweep` is refused naming `claims[i].sweep`. A `disputed` entry with `sweep`
  is refused naming `claims[i].sweep`. Refusals happen before any reviewer
  starts and record no result.
- **AC2. Sweep record.** `sweep` has exactly `class`, `search`, `sites`, `fix`,
  `test` and `dependents`. `class`, `search`, `fix`, `test` and `dependents`
  are non-empty text of at most 4096 characters. `sites` is a list of 1 to 256
  entries, each exactly `{path, line}` with non-empty text `path` of at most
  1024 characters and a positive integer `line`. A missing, empty, wrongly
  typed or out-of-bounds field, or an unknown key, is refused naming its path
  (for example `claims[0].sweep.search`, `claims[0].sweep.sites[1].line`). A
  record with one site and its search is accepted.
- **AC3. Records reach later rounds.** A boundary review (a selection-tagged
  `review` gate from `review.execute` or an individual `gate.execute`) whose
  inputs include sweep records receives `packet.sweepRecords` with `format`
  `sweep-record/1`, a `guide`, and `records`. Each record has `findingId`,
  `classId` (from the claimed finding in retained history), `round` (the
  round of the review that received the claim, or null outside the round
  policy) and the six sweep fields. The records are those of this
  execution's retained `fixed` claims plus those in the inputs of earlier
  boundary reviews of revisions in the same open review scope. Two records
  are duplicates when their `findingId`, `round` and six sweep fields are all
  equal (as when both peers of a round received the same claim); duplicates
  are carried once, while a finding claimed fixed again in a later round
  keeps both records. A claim dropped as `claim-not-retained` contributes no record.
  After the scope closes (integration, split or a reset by amendment), its
  records are not carried. Without any record, `packet.sweepRecords` is
  absent. The packet's `roundPolicy` also carries the reviewed `revision`.
- **AC4. A missed site of a swept class is a regression.** When a boundary
  review is qualified, a finding whose `classId` equals the `classId` of a
  sweep record of the open scope (from a round no later than the one being
  qualified), and whose ID differs from that record's `findingId`, is a
  regression of the previous fix. In rounds 4-5 a blocking medium finding of
  a swept class therefore blocks, whether it is new in this round or an
  earlier finding still `present`. A medium finding of a class no record
  sweeps is demoted as before; the claimed finding itself is not a class
  regression of its own sweep. Records of an open scope always carry a round
  (they come from round-policy reviews); a record with a null round appears
  only in its own packet and takes no part in qualification. GateReeve never
  changes a reviewer's `blocking` flag.
- **AC5. Reviewer guidance.** The reviewer instructions direct reviewers to
  `packet.sweepRecords.guide` when present. The guide tells reviewers to
  verify each record's sites, search, fix, test and dependents against the
  candidate; to report a missed site of a swept class with that record's
  `classId`; and that such a finding is a regression of the previous fix that
  blocks in rounds 4-5 when it is medium or high.
- **AC6. Peer re-reports and individual rounds (#134).** When qualifying
  findings, a review's re-report of an earlier finding ID (as a finding)
  counts as that finding still present in its round, like a `present`
  disposition, so a peer's `resolved` or `unsupported` in that round does not
  close it or clear its origin, in either processing order. Reviews whose
  inputs carry the round policy's scope, round and revision are grouped by
  those three values, so two individual selection-tagged reviews of one
  revision are one round; other reviews keep the existing grouping. In the
  #134 scenario (a medium regression raised in round 3, re-reported by one
  peer and marked `resolved` by the other in round 4, then FAILed on in round
  5 with every row passing) the round-5 review is FAIL.
- **AC7. Documentation.** `PROTOCOL.md` documents claim dispositions, the
  sweep record, the packet field and the regression rule.
  `commands/pr-review.md` tells the implementer how to write `fixed` and
  `disputed` claims. The desktop mirror is restaged.

